content.paragraph:
The preceding three examples involved diagonal matrices. To handle the nondiagonal case, we return for a moment to the $n \times n$ case in which eigenvectors of $A$ form a basis $\{\textbf{v}_{1},\ldots,\textbf{v}_{n}\}$ for $\textbf{ℝ}^{n}$. Let $P = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      \textbf{v}_{1} & \cdots & \textbf{v}_{n}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and let $D$ be the diagonal matrix with the corresponding eigenvalues on the diagonal. Given a sequence $\{\textbf{x}_{k}\}$ satisfying $\textbf{x}_{k + 1} = A\textbf{x}_{k}$, define a new sequence $\{\textbf{y}_{k}\}$ by

example.paragraph:
Using standard techniques, we find that $A$ has eigenvalues $2$ and $.5$, with corresponding eigenvectors $\textbf{v}_{1} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\textbf{v}_{2} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, respectively. Since $|2| > 1$ and $|.5| < 1$, the origin is a saddle point of the dynamical system. If $\textbf{x}_{0} = c_{1}\textbf{v}_{1} + c_{2}\textbf{v}_{2}$, then

has eigenvalues $.9 \pm .2i$, with eigenvectors $\begin{bmatrix}
      {1 \mp 2i} \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}$. Figure 5 shows three trajectories of the system $\textbf{x}_{k + 1} = A\textbf{x}_{k}$, with initial vectors $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
