statement.paragraph:
Let $A$ be a $2 \times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues $3$ and $1/3$ and corresponding eigenvectors $\textbf{v}_{1} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\textbf{v}_{2} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 1} \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Let $\{\textbf{x}_{k}\}$ be a solution of the difference equation $\textbf{x}_{k + 1} = A\textbf{x}_{k}$, $\textbf{x}_{0} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      9 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$.

Suppose the eigenvalues of a $3 \times 3$ matrix $A$ are $3$, $4/5$, and $3/5$, with corresponding eigenvectors $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      0 \\
      {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      1 \\
      {- 5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 3} \\
      {- 3} \\
      7
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Let $\textbf{x}_{0} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 2} \\
      {- 5} \\
      3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Find the solution of the equation $\textbf{x}_{k + 1} = A\textbf{x}_{k}$ for the specified $\textbf{x}_{0}$, and describe what happens as $k\rightarrow\text{∞}$.

In old-growth forests of Douglas fir, the spotted owl dines mainly on flying squirrels. Suppose the predator–prey matrix for these two populations is $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      .4 & .3 \\
      {- p} & {1.2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Show that if the predation parameter $p$ is $\text{.325}$, both populations grow. Estimate the long-term growth rate and the eventual ratio of owls to flying squirrels.

Let $A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      .4 & 0 & {\text{.}2} \\
      .3 & {\text{.}8} & {\text{.}3} \\
      .3 & {\text{.}2} & {\text{.}5}
      \end{bmatrix}$. The vector $\textbf{v}_{1} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      .1 \\
      .6 \\
      .3
      \end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector for $A$, and two eigenvalues are .5 and .2. Construct the solution of the dynamical system $\textbf{x}_{k + 1} = A\textbf{x}_{k}$ that satisfies $\textbf{x}_{0} = (0,.3,.7)$. What happens to $\textbf{x}_{k}$ as $k\rightarrow\text{∞}$?

[T] Produce the general solution of the dynamical system $\textbf{x}_{k + 1} = A\textbf{x}_{k}$ when $A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      .90 & .01 & {\text{.}09} \\
      .01 & {\text{.}90} & {\text{.}01} \\
      .09 & {\text{.}09} & {\text{.}90}
      \end{bmatrix}$.
